# gtools/mkblob.golfj — data/blob.hex -> the `` ` `` blob escape.
#
# The GOLF shadow of tools/mkblob2.blob_escape().  Authored in code-page
# mnemonic form and compiled by gtools/build (prelude, then every lib/t*.golfj,
# then this program).  Python stays the build path; this program is trusted
# exactly as far as test/gtools.sh shows it emits the SAME BYTES — see
# gtools/README.md.
#
# ===========================================================================
# Interface
# ===========================================================================
#
#     build/gmkblob data/blob.hex > /tmp/escape        # a path argument
#     build/gmkblob < data/blob.hex > /tmp/escape      # or a plain filter
#
# argv[1] if there is one, standard input otherwise.  Output always goes to
# stdout, diagnostics to fd 2, and a rejected input exits 1 with NOTHING on
# stdout (`a` deliberately does not flush the buffer — half a blob is worse
# than none of it, because half a blob still looks like a file).
#
# Both spellings, because both callers are real.  test/gtools.sh's `diff_filter`
# runs a tool as a bare stdin->stdout filter with no arguments, and the sibling
# codepage tools are filters by nature; but `mkblob` has exactly ONE meaningful
# input in the whole repo, and `gmkblob data/blob.hex` is what a Makefile rule
# or a shell one-liner wants to say.  Supporting the argument costs one line
# (`1 h`, tio's argv word, already answers 0 when there is no argv[1]) and
# closes off the failure mode where someone forgets the redirect and the tool
# sits waiting on a terminal.
#
# A SECOND argument is refused rather than ignored.  `gmkblob in.hex out.bin` is
# the mistake this shape invites — mkgolf2, the tool next door, really does take
# an output path — and ignoring the second word would write the blob to the
# terminal and look like it had worked.
#
# ===========================================================================
# What it emits
# ===========================================================================
#
# `mkblob2.blob_escape()` is
#
#     b"`" + str(len(blob)).encode() + b" " + blob
#
# — a backtick, the byte count in decimal, EXACTLY ONE space (the escape's
# delimiter, not padding), then that many raw bytes.  No trailing newline: the
# escape is spliced into the middle of self/golf2.golf at the @BLOB@ marker, so
# a stray byte on either side would land in the compiler's source.
#
# The blob itself is `[key][len][len bytes]` records followed by a single 0
# sentinel key.  data/blob.hex holds one whole record per line as space-
# separated uppercase hex pairs, in build_blob2()'s exact emission order with
# the M-VEC overrides already substituted, and — the one thing that is easy to
# get wrong — THE SENTINEL IS NOT IN THE FILE.  Its own header says so and says
# why: a bare `00` line would be the one line that is not a key+len+data record,
# so every reader would need a special case for the last line.  This program
# appends the 0 byte, and counts it in the length.
#
# NO COUNT IS BAKED IN — not the number of records, not the blob length, not the
# longest template.  data/blob.hex grows every time an op byte is allocated (it
# gained ⎈ \sys and ⌸ \brk in this wave alone), so the record count is read off
# the input, the buffer is sized from the input, and the decimal length is
# computed from what was actually decoded.  The figures in the file's own header
# — 55 records, 713 record bytes, 714 with the sentinel — are today's, and this
# program would emit tomorrow's without being touched.
#
# The output CONTAINS NUL BYTES and arbitrary binary — it is machine code — so
# nothing in the path below may treat a 0 byte as a terminator.  Nothing does:
# every byte goes out through tio's `g`, which is a counted buffer, and the
# record bytes are copied by index, never scanned for a terminator.
#
# ===========================================================================
# What it validates, and what it deliberately does not
# ===========================================================================
#
# A build tool that emitted a subtly wrong blob would poison the next bootstrap
# in a way the fixpoint might not even catch (a template that is never reached
# by the compiler's own source compiles clean and breaks a user's program).  So
# every structural property that is cheap to check is checked, and each one
# dies through `a` with its own message on fd 2 and exit status 1:
#
#   * the line is space-separated hex pairs — length must be 3n-1, so `A3 05 59`
#     passes and `A3 05 59 ` , `A3 0559` and `A3  05` do not;
#   * every pair is two hex digits (`l` answers -1 otherwise);
#   * every separator is ONE byte 0x20 — a tab or any other filler is rejected
#     even though the length arithmetic would have accepted it;
#   * at least two bytes on the line, i.e. a key and a length;
#   * THE DECLARED LENGTH EQUALS THE DATA — `len` byte == (bytes on line) - 2.
#     This is the check the whole file format exists to allow: a truncated line
#     and a hand-edited length are the two corruptions that would otherwise
#     produce a blob that is well-formed and wrong;
#   * the key is not 0 — a 0 key is the sentinel, and a record with one would
#     truncate the blob at that point for every reader;
#   * NO DUPLICATE KEYS.  The compiler's record lookup returns the FIRST match,
#     so a second record for a key is silently dead code — exactly the kind of
#     thing that survives every test until the day the two differ.  Worth the
#     256-byte table: duplicates are otherwise invisible;
#   * at least one record.  Zero records means empty input or the wrong file,
#     and `` `1 <NUL> `` is a perfectly well-formed escape for an empty blob,
#     which is the worst possible thing to emit silently.
#
# Deliberately NOT checked, because it is policy this program has no standing to
# hold an opinion about (data/blob.hex's own header: "a reader here is a
# formatter, not a policy engine"):
#
#   * the ORDER of the records, and that keys 1-5 come first.  Order is
#     load-bearing — the lookup returns the first match — but it is decided by
#     build_blob2() before the file is written, and test/selfcheck.sh already
#     fails if what is committed is not exactly what tools/mktables.py emits;
#   * that any particular key is present, or that the template bytes are valid
#     machine code.  There is no second source here to check them against;
#   * hex-digit CASE.  mktables.py writes upper case; `l` folds case, so lower
#     case is accepted.  Rejecting it would be a check on the generator's
#     formatting, not on the data.
#
# ===========================================================================
# Memory.  Eight fixed cells, and one heap block.
# ===========================================================================
# ../REGISTRY.md §3 leaves 0x4F00C0-0x4F00FF (5177536-5177599) free; this
# program uses all eight cells of it, 8-byte stride, `\fetch`/`\store`
# throughout.  That is a use, not a reservation: a gtools/ program is its own
# executable and the region is dead the moment it exits, so every tool here may
# spend the same eight cells however it likes.  Nothing is added to §3.
# This program uses NO ->x / <-x variable and never touches s0..s7.  The
# variable bank is one global cell per NAME, shared with anything else linked
# into the same binary, and it has changed width once already (W8A/M3W widened
# it to 8 bytes and made <-x a full 64-bit load, retiring the zero-extension
# hazard the tool libraries were written around); a fixed cell is 64-bit by
# construction, costs nothing, and cannot collide.  s0..s7 belong to the prelude
# and to the ttext words called below — every one of those frames itself with
# X/Y, so nothing here has to.
#
#   5177536  cur       the read cursor, an ADDRESS into the slurped input
#   5177544  rem       bytes left from cur (cursor + remaining, not base + index:
#                      two cells instead of three, and eight is the budget)
#   5177552  blobbuf   where decoded record bytes accumulate
#   5177560  bloblen   how many are there — also the running append offset
#   5177568  lineaddr  ) B's arguments; live only inside B, so main may and does
#   5177576  linelen   ) keep its own four across the call — then C's source
#   5177584  nb        bytes on the current line (B), then C's count
#   5177592  i         B's byte index, then B's key, then C's index
#
# B and C are the only two words this program defines, and B and C are the only
# two uppercase letters lib/prelude.golfj has left (its header: "Free: B and C —
# that is the entire remaining uppercase pool").  They never run at the same
# time — every record is parsed before the first byte is emitted — so the four
# cells above are shared rather than partitioned.
#
# ONE allocation, sized from the input (a record line is at least three bytes
# per record byte, so input/8 + 40 cells is always room and usually triple it):
#
#     base+0    .. base+255   the 256-byte seen-key table (zeroed explicitly)
#     base+256  .. base+287   the decimal scratch `o` formats the count into
#                             (20 bytes is the worst case; 32 is what it gets)
#     base+288  ..            blobbuf — the record bytes
#
# blobbuf is the only one of the three kept in a cell; the other two are spelled
# `blobbuf 288 \rsub` and `blobbuf 32 \rsub` at their use sites.  That is what
# makes eight cells enough.  The seen table is zeroed by hand rather than
# trusting fresh `brk` pages to be zero — they are, but a bump allocator that
# ever grows a reuse path would make that a silent wrong answer, and 256 stores
# cost nothing next to a syscall.
#
# ===========================================================================
# House rules inherited from lib/prelude.golfj and the tool libraries
# ===========================================================================
#   * Raw atoms only for every pointer, index, count and flag step: \radd \rsub
#     \rmul \rmin \rlt.  The bare ASCII + - * are polymorphic since M-VEC and
#     would send this program's own address math through the shape dispatcher.
#     `/` is not in the hook table and stays bare, and is UNSIGNED, which is
#     what the (linelen+1)/3 arithmetic wants.
#   * Flag polarity is zero-is-true, like `[` itself.  \eq yields -1 when equal
#     and 0 when not, so `<x> <y> \eq [ die ]` is "die unless they match" with
#     no adapter; the -1/0 of \slt and \rlt is turned round with `1\radd` where
#     a block has to run on TRUE instead.
#   * `{…}` is a DO-while, so its body always runs once.  The line loop is
#     guarded with `<rem> 0\eq [ {…} ]` (an empty input has no lines); B's
#     per-byte loop is not, because B is only called with nb >= 2; C's is not,
#     and C is therefore documented as needing n >= 1 — both its callers pass a
#     count already proved non-zero (`o` returns at least 1 even for the value
#     0, and the "no records" check has already run).
#   * Byte 0x5C (swap) never appears — `decode` renders it as a bare `\`, which
#     `encode` would then read as a mnemonic escape.  Where an operand order
#     needed reversing it went through a cell (B's key) or through `&` (over).
#   * `g` and `)` are two different streams; this program uses `g` for the
#     artifact and `a` (fd 2) for anything it has to say about it, and never
#     `)`, `N`, `O` or `Q`.
# ===========================================================================

# B : one record line -> validated bytes appended to blobbuf.  addr len ->
# The line is 3n-1 bytes: n hex pairs with n-1 single spaces between them, so
# byte i is at addr+3i and the separator before it at addr+3i-1.  `l` reads two
# bytes unconditionally, and the last pair ends at addr+3n-2 = addr+linelen-1,
# so the sweep stops exactly at the end of the line and never past it.
:B 5177576\store 5177568\store
5177576\fetch 1\radd 3/ 5177584\store
5177584\fetch 3\rmul 1\rsub 5177576\fetch\eq[“mkblob: record line is not space-separated hex pairs“a]
5177584\fetch 2\rlt 1\radd[“mkblob: record line is shorter than a key and a length byte“a]
0 5177592\store
{5177592\fetch 0\eq[5177568\fetch 5177592\fetch 3\rmul\radd 1\rsub?32\eq[“mkblob: hex pairs must be separated by exactly one space“a]]
5177568\fetch 5177592\fetch 3\rmul\radd l"0\slt 1\radd[“mkblob: not a pair of hex digits“a]
5177552\fetch 5177560\fetch\radd 5177592\fetch\radd,
5177592\fetch 1\radd 5177592\store 5177592\fetch 5177584\fetch\rlt 1\radd}
# The record is in the buffer; now the three checks that need it whole.  The
# length byte is blobbuf[bloblen+1] and must equal nb-2, the key is
# blobbuf[bloblen] and is parked in 5177592 (the index is finished with) so that
# the seen-table address can be built above it without a swap.
5177552\fetch 5177560\fetch\radd 1\radd?5177584\fetch 2\rsub\eq[“mkblob: the length byte disagrees with the data on the line“a]
5177552\fetch 5177560\fetch\radd?5177592\store
5177592\fetch 0\eq 1\radd[“mkblob: key 0 is the sentinel and cannot start a record“a]
5177552\fetch 288\rsub 5177592\fetch\radd?0\eq[“mkblob: duplicate record key would shadow the later record“a]
1 5177552\fetch 288\rsub 5177592\fetch\radd,
5177560\fetch 5177584\fetch\radd 5177560\store;

# C : emit n bytes from addr through tio's buffered `g`.  addr n ->
# The count is gospel and a 0 byte is just another byte, so this is the one
# thing between the blob and stdout that could have gone wrong on binary and
# does not: no terminator is looked for and none is written.  UNGUARDED — a
# do-while, so n must be at least 1; both callers have already proved theirs.
:C 5177584\store 5177568\store 0 5177592\store
{5177568\fetch 5177592\fetch\radd?g 5177592\fetch 1\radd 5177592\store 5177592\fetch 5177584\fetch\rlt 1\radd};

# Open the input and slurp it.  `2 h` is argv[2] or 0, and a second argument is
# a mistake, not an output path.  `1 h` is argv[1] or 0; a non-zero pointer is
# NUL-terminated by the kernel, which is what open(2) wants, so `0 b` opens it
# read-only.  A zero pointer IS the fd this program then reads: 0, stdin — the
# two cases share the cell, which is why there is no branch around `e`.
2 h 0\eq[“mkblob: takes at most one argument, the file to read; output is stdout“a]
1 h"0\eq[0 b]e 5177544\store 5177536\store

# One heap block: seen table, decimal scratch, then the record bytes.
5177544\fetch 8/ 40\radd A 288\radd 5177552\store
0 5177560\store
0 5177592\store{0 5177552\fetch 288\rsub 5177592\fetch\radd,5177592\fetch 1\radd 5177592\store 5177592\fetch 256\rlt 1\radd}

# The line loop.  `q` finds the newline; -1 (no newline before EOF, i.e. an
# unterminated last line) means the line is all that is left.  The cursor then
# advances by min(linelen+1, rem), which is linelen+1 for a terminated line and
# rem for that last one — one expression instead of carrying the -1 forward, and
# it can never be 0, so the loop always makes progress.  cur/rem are updated
# BEFORE the line is handled, so lineaddr and linelen can stay on the data stack
# (where a -1 is still a -1) and B is free to use the cells it needs.
5177544\fetch 0\eq[{
5177536\fetch"5177544\fetch 10 q"0\slt 1\radd[_5177544\fetch]
"1\radd 5177544\fetch\rmin"5177536\fetch\radd 5177536\store 5177544\fetch&\rsub 5177544\store_
"0\eq[&?35\eq[&&B]]_ _
5177544\fetch 0\eq}]
5177560\fetch 0\eq 1\radd[“mkblob: no records in the input“a]

# The escape.  Backtick, the count IN DECIMAL — bloblen plus the one sentinel
# byte — one space, the record bytes, the sentinel, and the final `f` that is
# the flush nothing else can know is due.  `o` writes the digits into the
# scratch at blobbuf-32 and returns how many, which has to be parked so that the
# address can be pushed under it for C.
96 g
5177560\fetch 1\radd 5177552\fetch 32\rsub o 5177584\store
5177552\fetch 32\rsub 5177584\fetch C
32 g
5177552\fetch 5177560\fetch C
0 g f
